      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://brennan.day/assets/images/blog/reload.jpg\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://brennan.day/assets/images/blog/reload.jpg\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://brennan.day/assets/images/blog/reload.jpg\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\nRevisiting my maladaptive daydream of waking up at twelve years old with all my present knowledge intact, I weigh the seductive fantasy of correcting every regret—being a better brother to Byron, buying Bitcoin with birthday money—against everything that would be erased: 500,000 words, hard-won relationships, the album that got me through a specific winter. A meditation on the future self who would trade almost anything for one more ordinary day. The door is already halfway closed.",
