      "id": "https://www.themarginalian.org/2026/09/15/tolkien-faery/",
      "url": "https://www.themarginalian.org/2026/09/15/tolkien-faery/",
      "title": "[The Marginalian] J.R.R. Tolkien on How to Fall in Love with Life",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://www.themarginalian.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/tolkien_vro.jpg?fit=1200%2C632&amp;ssl=1\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://www.themarginalian.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/tolkien_vro.jpg?fit=1200%2C632&amp;ssl=1\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://www.themarginalian.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/tolkien_vro.jpg?fit=1200%2C632&amp;ssl=1\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://www.themarginalian.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/tolkien_vro.jpg?fit=1200%2C632&amp;ssl=1\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://www.themarginalian.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/tolkien_vro.jpg?fit=1200%2C632&amp;ssl=1\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\nIn 1964, Pantheon Books invited J.R.R. Tolkien (January 3, 1892&#8211;September 2, 1973) to write an introduction to George MacDonald&#8217;s The Golden Key, which he had praised in Tree and Leaf &#8212; the small, splendid essay collection that gave us his uncommonly insightful meditation on fairy tales, the language of fantasy, and why there is no such thing as writing &#8220;for children.&#8221; He did love the book, so he agreed. But after two years of uneasy work on it, he finally conceded it was a betrayal of his lifelong credo that editorial introductions are an intrusion on the reader&#8217;s joy in&#8230;&#160;<a class=\"h3 read_on\" href=\"https://www.themarginalian.org/2026/09/15/tolkien-faery/\">read&#160;article</a>",
